    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22830": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Interior Design",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Interior Design Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant/Associate Professor, Interior Design Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested individuals are encouraged to submit their credentials as soon as possible for full consideration, and include: A cover letter clearly addressing interest in and qualifications for the position, including a statement about the candidate's commitment to design and teaching A current curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching excellence A media file of up to 20 images of student work with descriptions for each (PDF) Portfolio with evidence of the candidate's creative or scholarly work (PDF). The names, emails, and phone numbers for 3 references. Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) All applications and nominations will be handled in confidence. Questions about the position and nominations should be directed to Department of Design Chair, Jason Tselentis: tselentisj@winthrop.edu. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Interior Design Job Description Winthrop University, a highly ranked, public, comprehensive university, announces a search for an Assistant/Associate Professor of Interior Design. This is a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track appointment in the Department of Design within the College of Visual and Performing Arts. Our campus is in Rock Hill, South Carolina approximately 30 minutes from Charlotte, NC which has a thriving design market and international airport. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work in and develop our new state of the art facilities for Interior Design at The Thread, a dynamic and flexible hub for learning, collaboration, and creativity, representing a transformative initiative aimed at enhancing the educational environment for students, faculty, and the wider community. This position has an August 2027 start date. Winthrop University seeks an experienced interior designer and/or architect with a terminal degree in their field, and a proven record of relevant professional experience that aligns with contemporary principles and practices. The ideal candidate will have a strong emphasis on human-centered design methodologies. They will be an innovative teacher and designer, producer of impactful creative and/or scholarly work, as well as leaders in meaningful service activities for multiple communities. We are seeking a candidate who is committed to student success, impact, and access, by fostering an environment that prioritizes excellence. Position Responsibilities This position requires in person, daily contact with students, faculty, staff, and community, on campus and in our downtown Design Center which is a short walk from the main campus. Teach 3–4 classes per semester, or 18 contact hours. Teach all levels in the undergraduate Bachelor of Design in Interior Design degree program. Advise and mentor undergraduate Interior Design majors in professional and career preparation. Participate in curriculum development, accreditation, assessment, and course planning. Maintain creative and scholarly activity. Fully support and participate in the department’s recruitment efforts. Engage in professional service to the department, college, university, discipline, and community. Seek and maintain relationships with community and industry partners to support student internships, applied design projects, and our programs. Assume other responsibilities according to departmental needs and candidate’s qualifications. About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Candidates should hold a terminal degree in Interior Design, Interior Architecture, or Architecture. Candidates holding a bachelor’s degree within these disciplines, with significant teaching and professional experience deemed equivalent to a terminal degree may be considered. Proficiency with industry-standard software (e.g. AutoCAD, Revitt, SketchUp, and/or Bluebeam), as well as experience creating contract documentation using 3D software. Record of teaching, scholarship, and professional activity. Evidence of teaching excellence. Willingness to collaborate, communicate, and contribute to the growth of the department. Preferred Qualifications National Council for Interior Design Qualification ( NCIDQ ) certification or eligibility to pass the NCIDQ exam. Minimally, three to five years professional practice in Interior Design, Architecture, or a closely related professional field. Minimally, two years of university-level teaching experience. Technology Integration such as Building Information Modeling ( BIM ), virtual reality (VR), or other digital tools that enhance the design process. Awareness of and ability to teach Environmentally Conscious Design. Employment Conditions Full-time, tenure-track faculty member, 9-month appointment beginning 2027 August 16th. Application Instructions Interested individuals are encouraged to submit their credentials as soon as possible for full consideration, and include: A cover letter clearly addressing interest in and qualifications for the position, including a statement about the candidate’s commitment to design and teaching A current curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching excellence A media file of up to 20 images of student work with descriptions for each ( PDF ) Portfolio with evidence of the candidate’s creative or scholarly work ( PDF ). The names, emails, and phone numbers for 3 references. Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) All applications and nominations will be handled in confidence. Questions about the position and nominations should be directed to Department of Design Chair, Jason Tselentis: tselentisj@winthrop.edu. Open Date 09/11/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0013P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty-Evidence of teaching excellence Media File Portfolio Faculty - List of References Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22842": {
      "title": "Instructor, Chemistry",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Instructor, Chemistry Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Instructor, Chemistry Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The application deadline is October 23, 2026. Materials received after this date may be considered if an acceptable candidate has not been found. Applicants must apply electronically and submit all required documents by the closing date. Applications must include: A letter of interest. Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested. Graduate and undergraduate transcripts. (Unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the application; official transcripts will be required later for an offer of employment.) A detailed statement of teaching philosophy and experience, including interests for courses to be taught at Winthrop. A brief statement discussing professional service responsibilities that may be of interest, consistent with the department priorities listed above. Please describe related experience, if any, and relevant skills. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of all references.) For questions about the position, please contact: Dr. Robin Lammi, Chair Department of Chemistry Winthrop University Rock Hill, SC 29733 Tel: 803.323.4946 Lammir@winthrop.edu Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Instructor, Chemistry Job Description Winthrop University is seeking applicants with expertise in organic or biochemistry for a multi-year, non-tenure-track Instructor of Chemistry position, with employment to begin August 2027. The Chemistry Department ( https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/chemistry/ ) hosts ACS -approved and ASBMB -accredited chemistry and biochemistry undergraduate programs focused on student professional development and career readiness. The department offers small classes with rigorous, innovative curricula and emphasizes year-round engagement in national-caliber undergraduate research. The successful candidate will join a collegial team of 10 other chemistry/biochemistry faculty and two support scientists housed in modern facilities, including newly renovated teaching labs and excellent instrumentation for teaching and research. Review of applications will begin on October 23, 2026. Application materials received after this date may be considered if an acceptable candidate has not been found. Position Responsibilities Develop and teach undergraduate courses in General Chemistry lecture and lab, as well as introductory-level organic or biochemistry courses. The latter may include introductory GOB courses (i.e., survey of General-Organic-Biochemistry). Provide professional service to students, the department, university, and scientific community in areas of mutual interest. Responsibilities could include one or more of the following, depending on the candidate’s interests and strengths: coordinating retention and/or assessment efforts for introductory chemistry courses; leading the department tutoring program; advising student organizations; coordinating industrial internship opportunities; and/or leading K-12 outreach efforts. About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications MS and/or PhD including 18 graduate hours in chemistry, biochemistry, or closely related discipline Strong background and demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate General Chemistry lecture and lab, as well as introductory organic or biochemistry coursework Exceptional interpersonal skills and strong personal ethics for mentorship of students, for working in a team-oriented environment, and for sparking enthusiasm for chemistry Demonstrated passion for teaching, curriculum development, and working with students Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of chemistry/biochemistry teaching experience is preferred, ideally as instructor of record (i.e., lead instructor) Employment Conditions Full-time, multi-year, non-tenure-track appointment; renewable, with instructional duties during the academic years (9 months, Aug. 16 – May 15), and the option to teach (or not) during the summers for additional compensation. Application Instructions The application deadline is October 23, 2026. Materials received after this date may be considered if an acceptable candidate has not been found. Applicants must apply electronically and submit all required documents by the closing date. Applications must include: A letter of interest. Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested. Graduate and undergraduate transcripts. (Unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the application; official transcripts will be required later for an offer of employment.) A detailed statement of teaching philosophy and experience, including interests for courses to be taught at Winthrop. A brief statement discussing professional service responsibilities that may be of interest, consistent with the department priorities listed above. Please describe related experience, if any, and relevant skills. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of all references.) For questions about the position, please contact: Dr. Robin Lammi, Chair Department of Chemistry Winthrop University Rock Hill, SC 29733 Tel: 803.323.4946 Lammir@winthrop.edu Open Date 09/11/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0012P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty - Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Faculty - Statement Discuss Professional Service Responsibilities Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22860": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit 1) a letter of application stating candidate’s qualifications for the position and teaching philosophy; 2) a current CV including the names, phone numbers, and email address of three professional references from whom letters of recommendation can be requested; 3) a sample of representative writing (an article, or chapter-length contribution); and 4) all graduate transcripts (unofficial are acceptable for initial review). All materials should be submitted by October 19, 2026. Please contact Dr. Amanda Hiner at hinera@winthrop.edu with questions. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise Job Description The Department of English at Winthrop University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position specializing in Medieval Literature. The successful candidate will have teaching and research expertise in medieval literature cultures and be able to contribute to a curriculum that brings medieval texts into conversations with later literary traditions, global contexts, and diverse forms of storytelling. Teaching responsibilities will support undergraduate and graduate programs, including significant contributions to Winthrop’s innovative general education core (Composition, The Human Experience, and Critical Reading, Thinking, and Writing). This individual must have both a strong teaching record and a promising research agenda. Position Responsibilities Teach courses in their area of specialization as well as contribute to the department’s comprehensive undergraduate and graduate curriculum. Teaching responsibilities may include Chaucer, Shakespeare, World Literature, and other courses in medieval and early modern literature and culture. The successful candidate will also contribute to the Medieval Studies minor, including developing and teaching courses that explore a range of medieval and early modern texts, genres, traditions, and forms of storytelling. As part of the department’s broader teaching mission, the successful candidate will also contribute to the university’s general education curriculum, which may include WRIT 101, HMXP , and CRTW . We prefer candidates who can help students encounter the medieval world through an expansive understanding of literary culture and who can make connections across periods, traditions, media, and cultures. Possible areas of interest might include, but are not limited to, manuscript and book history; gender and sexuality; race and ethnicity; religion; material culture; environmental humanities; digital humanities; adaptation and reception; global or comparative medieval studies; and histories and theories of narrative. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to excellent undergraduate teaching, an active scholarly agenda, and collaborative participation in the intellectual and curricular life of the department and university. Major responsibilities include: Teaching the equivalent of twelve contact hours each semester, including those in the general education core, as well as upper-division courses and graduate courses in the area of specialization. Developing curricula and new courses for the B.A. and M.A. programs. Pursuing productive research in the discipline and promote undergraduate and graduate research. Advising and mentoring English majors. Participating in professional service activities for the department, college, university, and discipline. Other duties as assigned by the department chair. About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in English, Medieval Studies, or a closely related field by the appointment date. ABD candidates will be considered and appointed at the rank of instructor until the doctorate is completed Specialization in Medieval Literature. Evidence of effective college-level teaching or potential for excellence in teaching. Evidence of an active scholarly agenda appropriate to a tenure-track position. Strong potential for contributions to WRIT 101, HMXP , and CRTW Interpersonal skills required for successful collaboration with colleagues and students. Preferred Qualifications Ability or interest in teaching Chaucer and a range of medieval literature. Ability to teach Shakespeare and/or other early modern literature. Ability to contribute to World Literature offerings Interest in developing interdisciplinary courses and contributing to the continued development of the Medieval Studies minor. Teaching or scholarship that expands students’ understanding of medieval and early modern literary cultures through global, comparative, interdisciplinary, or innovative approaches to texts and storytelling. Employment Conditions This is a full-time, tenure-track appointment requiring daily on-campus contact; instructional duties during the academic year (9 months, Aug. 16 – May 15) come with the option to teach during the summers for additional compensation. Application Instructions Applicants should submit 1) a letter of application stating candidate’s qualifications for the position and teaching philosophy; 2) a current CV including the names, phone numbers, and email address of three professional references from whom letters of recommendation can be requested; 3) a sample of representative writing (an article, or chapter-length contribution); and 4) all graduate transcripts (unofficial are acceptable for initial review). All materials should be submitted by October 19, 2026. Please contact Dr. Amanda Hiner at hinera@winthrop.edu with questions. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Open Date 09/15/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0016P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty-Letter of application and statement of teaching philosophy Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22827": {
      "title": "SBDC Region Director",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | SBDC Region Director Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources SBDC Region Director Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title SBDC Region Director State Title Director State Class Code UP03 State Pay Table Annual Salary/Hiring Range $80,000 Job Purpose Reporting to the Winthrop University Dean of Business & Technology and operationally to the South Carolina Small Business Development Center (SC SBDC ) State Director, the Region Director serves as the SC SBDC’s primary representative to the host institution, as well as business and government leaders, and oversees personnel, business consulting services, program compliance and performance, strategic planning, continuing education initiatives and public relations efforts. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree. Professional experience managing multiple departments, personnel and operating budgets. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in business administration or a relevant field. Experience in entrepreneurial, small-business ownership, business consulting, strategic planning, program administration, financial oversight and stakeholder engagement. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Summary Knowledge of small business development, entrepreneurship, strategic planning, program administration, budgeting and financial management. Knowledge of federal, state, university and SC SBDC policies, procedures and compliance requirements and the ability to interpret and apply them. Strong leadership and supervisory skills, including the ability to establish, monitor and coach performance expectations. Effective oral and written communication skills. Exceptional consulting and presentation skills. Ability to analyze performance and financial data. Ability to manage multiple offices, departments, personnel and competing priorities. Ability to build effective relationships with business owners, university leadership, government officials, community partners and other stakeholders. Ability to exercise sound judgment, work independently, and maintain a high level of professionalism, confidentiality and accountability. Job Summary Program Management Directs all regional operations of the SC SBDC program, including personnel, business planning and management, program compliance and related operations. Coordinates continuing education programs to address market needs. Ensures compliance with applicable federal, state, university, and SC SBDC laws, regulations, policies, and ASBDC Accreditation Standards. Assists the State Director with program compliance, statewide initiative development and implementation, strategic planning, policy and procedure administration, accreditation preparation, and resolution of management issues. Client Consulting and Training Manages area offices within the region and supervises employee performance. Provides high-quality counseling and consulting services to prospective and existing small business owners. Conducts and participates in client training programs, workshops and educational initiatives. Supports the SC SBDC technology commercialization program in collaboration with the Innovation Center serving the greater York County area by identifying commercialization opportunities, connecting clients with technical and financial resources, and developing partnerships that promote innovation and business growth. Budget Administration and Fiscal Management Manages the regional operating budget, including annual budget development in coordination with the SC SBDC administrative center at the University of South Carolina. Conducts financial analyses and monitors expenditures to ensure funds are allocated and utilized effectively and within budget. Prepares and submits required reports. Community Outreach and Public Relations (PR) Serves as an official representative of the College of Business & Technology and the SC SBDC , planning and overseeing outreach, public relations and stakeholder engagement activities. Promotes awareness and utilization of SC SBDC services through collaboration with university departments, elected officials and the regional business community. Develops and maintains strategic partnerships that leverage resources to support small business growth and maximize program outcomes. Establishes and maintains cooperative working relationships within the College of Business & Technology and with other university departments to advance initiatives. Represents the SC SBDC at community, business and economic development events to strengthen visibility and stakeholder engagement. Ensures outreach and partnership activities align with SC SBDC goals, priorities and strategic objectives. Travel/Overtime Requirements Occasional travel and evenings and weekend hours. Telecommuting Employees are not eligible for telecommuting/remote work during their 12-month probationary period. After that time, certain positions may be eligible for telecommuting/remote work at the discretion of the supervisor and in accordance with Winthrop policies. License/Certification Type of Position Unclassified Position Status Full-Time FLSA Status Exempt Hours Per Week 37.5 Months per Year 12 Posting Detail Information Posting Number 2027026S Open Date 09/10/2026 Close Date 09/24/2026 Special Instructions to Applicants PLEASE NOTE * A resume will not be reviewed to determine whether an applicant meets the qualifications outlined in the job vacancy notice (posting). * Responses to supplemental questions are considered part of the application; failure to respond will result in disqualification. NOTICE Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * List your areas of business expertise. (Open Ended Question) * Describe in detail your experience managing budgets and maintaining financial records, including the size of the department/organization and the number/size of the budget(s). (Open Ended Question) * Provide an example that illustrates your ability to successfully network and/or build partnerships with external constituents. (Open Ended Question) * Indicate the size and composition of the largest team you managed directly and summarize your leadership style. (Open Ended Question) Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22832": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Graphic Design",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Graphic Design Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant/Associate Professor, Graphic Design Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications should include: Cover letter addressing the candidate’s qualifications and experience in relation to the responsibilities and requirements of the position. Statement of teaching philosophy outlining the candidate’s approach to teaching and learning in Graphic Design. Current curriculum vitae. Media file with teaching portfolio demonstrating evidence of teaching excellence, including up to 20 examples of student work. Each example should include a brief description identifying the course, project or assignment, and relevant context. Portfolio materials may be submitted as a PDF or URL as a media file. Portfolio of up to 20 examples of the candidate's professional, creative, or scholarly work, submitted as a PDF or URL. Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) All applications and nominations will be handled in confidence. Questions about the position and nominations should be directed to Department of Design Chair, Jason Tselentis: tselentisj@winthrop.edu Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Graphic Design Job Description Winthrop University, a highly ranked, public, comprehensive university, announces a search for a full-time tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor of Graphic Design. This nine-month appointment is in the Department of Design within the College of Visual and Performing Arts. Our campus is in Rock Hill, South Carolina, approximately thirty minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina’s thriving design and art market and international airport. This position requires daily contact with students, faculty, and staff on campus. Candidates should demonstrate commitment to an interdisciplinary and collaborative approach to design studies, at the foundation, intermediate, and advanced levels. The ideal candidate will demonstrate expertise in design practices including graphic design layout, design research and design thinking, interactive and web design, and interactive digital products. Position Responsibilities Teaching 15–18 contact hours a semester (3 studio classes) Teach all levels in the Graphic Design Program Participate in the strategic assessment, development, and advancement of the Graphic Design Program and its facilities Contribute to the development and growth of Graphic Design teaching and learning spaces, studios, and related resources to support evolving pedagogical and program needs Participate in curriculum development, accreditation, and annual assessment Advise and mentor undergraduate Design majors Engage in creative and scholarly activity Fully support and participate in the department’s recruitment efforts Engage in professional service to the department, college, university, discipline, and community Seek and maintain relationships with community and industry partners to support student internships, applied design projects, and our programs Assume other responsibilities according to departmental needs and candidate’s qualifications About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Candidates should hold a terminal degree ( MFA , MDes,) in Graphic Design or a related Design discipline. Candidates holding a bachelor’s degree in graphic design, visual communication (or commensurate)—with significant teaching and/or professional experience equivalent to a terminal degree— may be considered. Candidates should have advanced knowledge of Adobe Creative Cloud, layout tools such as Illustrator and InDesign, and experience with industry-standard tools such as Microsoft Office and Figma. Candidates should understand user experience design, for digital apps and products, as well as an understanding of typographic composition for print and digital media across communication systems, branding and experiential design. Their teaching portfolio and own professional design work should show exemplary skills in those areas. Candidates should have a record of and evidence teaching excellence, scholarship, and professional activity. Candidates should demonstrate a willingness and ability to work collaboratively with faculty and students, communicate effectively, and contribute to a collegial and engaged learning environment. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to contributing to the continued growth and development of the Graphic Design Program and department. Preferred Qualifications Minimum of three years of university-level teaching experience, which could include graduate-level and adjunct teaching. Minimum of three years of professional experience in Industry. Employment Conditions Full-time, tenure-track, 9-month appointment Application Instructions Applications should include: Cover letter addressing the candidate’s qualifications and experience in relation to the responsibilities and requirements of the position. Statement of teaching philosophy outlining the candidate’s approach to teaching and learning in Graphic Design. Current curriculum vitae. Media file with teaching portfolio demonstrating evidence of teaching excellence, including up to 20 examples of student work. Each example should include a brief description identifying the course, project or assignment, and relevant context. Portfolio materials may be submitted as a PDF or URL as a media file. Portfolio of up to 20 examples of the candidate’s professional, creative, or scholarly work, submitted as a PDF or URL . Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) All applications and nominations will be handled in confidence. Questions about the position and nominations should be directed to Department of Design Chair, Jason Tselentis: tselentisj@winthrop.edu Open Date 09/11/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0014P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Media File Portfolio Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
