    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22802": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Master of Arts in Arts Administration (MAAA) Online and Capstone Advisor Pool",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct Instructor, Master of Arts in Arts Administration (MAAA) Online and Capstone Advisor Pool Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct Instructor, Master of Arts in Arts Administration (MAAA) Online and Capstone Advisor Pool Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Qualified and interested applicants are asked to apply via PeopleAdmin and upload the following documents: Letter of interest (cover letter) Curriculum vitae or resume Unofficial transcripts (official transcripts required by the start of the appointment) In the letter of interest (cover letter), applicants are encouraged to identify the subject areas they are most qualified to teach or advise, as well as any experience with online teaching, graduate instruction, student advising, capstone supervision, arts administration, or nonprofit arts leadership. Applications will be reviewed as program needs arise. Inclusion in the applicant pool does not guarantee employment or a specific teaching or advising assignment. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct Instructor, Master of Arts in Arts Administration (MAAA) Online and Capstone Advisor Pool Job Description Winthrop University’s College of Visual and Performing Arts ( CVPA ) seeks qualified applicants to be considered for a hiring pool of online Adjunct Instructors and Capstone Advisors in the Master of Arts in Arts Administration ( MAAA ) program. The MAAA program is a 100% online graduate program serving working professionals, emerging arts leaders, and individuals preparing for leadership roles in arts and cultural organizations. The program includes coursework in areas such as arts administration, leadership and management, marketing, financial management, fundraising and development, community engagement, cultural policy and advocacy, arts law, sustainability, research, and capstone development. A hiring pool means that qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for possible future teaching, advising, or instructional support opportunities as program needs arise. Placement in the pool does not guarantee a course assignment, advising assignment, or specific semester of employment. Adjunct teaching and capstone advising opportunities are based on program needs, enrollment, subject-matter fit, and University approval processes. About the Master of Arts in Arts Administration Program Winthrop University’s Master of Arts in Arts Administration is a fully online graduate program designed for students who want to build practical, ethical, and sustainable leadership skills for work in arts and cultural organizations. The program emphasizes applied learning, professional relevance, and connections between theory and practice. Students complete coursework across major areas of arts administration and conclude the program with a capstone experience that allows them to investigate a significant issue, challenge, or opportunity in the field. Position Responsibilities Responsibilities may include: Teaching assigned graduate course(s) online using the University’s learning management system, Blackboard Designing, updating, and facilitating high-quality online learning experiences, including weekly modules, discussions, feedback, assessments, and video lectures Providing timely communication, grading, and student support Aligning course outcomes, activities, and assessments with program expectations Collaborating with the Program Director on course planning, consistency, student support, and continuous improvement Maintaining appropriate academic documentation, including syllabi, grade records, and assessment artifacts Serving as a Capstone Advisor for graduate students completing applied research or professional projects in arts administration Providing feedback on capstone proposals, research development, literature reviews, project design, drafts, and final products Supporting students as they connect research, professional practice, and arts administration leadership Possible Areas of Instruction or Advising Applicants may be considered for teaching or advising in one or more areas related to arts administration, including but not limited to: Arts administration and nonprofit arts leadership Arts management Marketing and audience development Financial management, budgeting, and fiscal planning Fundraising, development, and grant writing Community engagement and public programming Cultural policy and advocacy Arts law, contracts, and governance Organizational sustainability Strategic planning Research methods and applied capstone development Arts entrepreneurship Equity, access, inclusion, and community-centered arts practice Museum, theatre, music, dance, visual arts, or interdisciplinary arts administration About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Not applicable Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 01/01/2026 Required Qualifications Adjunct faculty teaching graduate courses at Winthrop University must have a master’s degree or higher in the teaching discipline or a closely related discipline. A terminal degree, such as an MFA , Ph.D., Ed.D., D.M.A., J.D., M.B.A., or other relevant terminal credential, is preferred. Preferred Qualifications Preferred qualifications include: Professional experience in arts administration, nonprofit arts leadership, cultural organizations, higher education, or a related field Experience teaching, training, mentoring, or advising adult learners Experience with online teaching or online learning environments Familiarity with Blackboard or another learning management system Ability to provide clear, timely, and constructive feedback to graduate students Experience supporting applied research, capstone projects, professional writing, or project-based learning Commitment to accessible, inclusive, and student-centered online instruction Employment Conditions Position is part-time, non-tenure-track, adjunct. Application Instructions Qualified and interested applicants are asked to apply via PeopleAdmin and upload the following documents: Letter of interest (cover letter) Curriculum vitae or resume Unofficial transcripts (official transcripts required by the start of the appointment) In the letter of interest (cover letter), applicants are encouraged to identify the subject areas they are most qualified to teach or advise, as well as any experience with online teaching, graduate instruction, student advising, capstone supervision, arts administration, or nonprofit arts leadership. Applications will be reviewed as program needs arise. Inclusion in the applicant pool does not guarantee employment or a specific teaching or advising assignment. Open Date 09/08/2026 Application Review Date 09/08/2026 Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0011P PositionType: Adjunct Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Transcripts Optional Documents Resume Curriculum Vitae IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22867": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit 1) a letter of application stating candidate’s qualifications for the position and teaching philosophy; 2) a current CV including the names, phone numbers, and email address of three professional references from whom letters of recommendation can be requested; 3) a sample of representative writing (an article, or chapter-length contribution); and 4) all graduate transcripts (unofficial are acceptable for initial review). All materials should be submitted by October 19, 2026. Please contact Dr. Amanda Hiner at hinera@winthrop.edu with questions. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor, English-Medieval literature expertise Job Description The Department of English at Winthrop University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position specializing in Medieval Literature. The successful candidate will have teaching and research expertise in medieval literature cultures and be able to contribute to a curriculum that brings medieval texts into conversations with later literary traditions, global contexts, and diverse forms of storytelling. Teaching responsibilities will support undergraduate and graduate programs, including significant contributions to Winthrop’s innovative general education core (Composition, The Human Experience, and Critical Reading, Thinking, and Writing). This individual must have both a strong teaching record and a promising research agenda. Position Responsibilities Teach courses in their area of specialization as well as contribute to the department’s comprehensive undergraduate and graduate curriculum. Teaching responsibilities may include Chaucer, Shakespeare, World Literature, and other courses in medieval and early modern literature and culture. The successful candidate will also contribute to the Medieval Studies minor, including developing and teaching courses that explore a range of medieval and early modern texts, genres, traditions, and forms of storytelling. As part of the department’s broader teaching mission, the successful candidate will also contribute to the university’s general education curriculum, which may include WRIT 101, HMXP , and CRTW . We prefer candidates who can help students encounter the medieval world through an expansive understanding of literary culture and who can make connections across periods, traditions, media, and cultures. Possible areas of interest might include, but are not limited to, manuscript and book history; gender and sexuality; race and ethnicity; religion; material culture; environmental humanities; digital humanities; adaptation and reception; global or comparative medieval studies; and histories and theories of narrative. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to excellent undergraduate teaching, an active scholarly agenda, and collaborative participation in the intellectual and curricular life of the department and university. Major responsibilities include: Teaching the equivalent of twelve contact hours each semester, including those in the general education core, as well as upper-division courses and graduate courses in the area of specialization. Developing curricula and new courses for the B.A. and M.A. programs. Pursuing productive research in the discipline and promote undergraduate and graduate research. Advising and mentoring English majors. Participating in professional service activities for the department, college, university, and discipline. Other duties as assigned by the department chair. About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in English, Medieval Studies, or a closely related field by the appointment date. ABD candidates will be considered and appointed at the rank of instructor until the doctorate is completed Specialization in Medieval Literature. Evidence of effective college-level teaching or potential for excellence in teaching. Evidence of an active scholarly agenda appropriate to a tenure-track position. Strong potential for contributions to WRIT 101, HMXP , and CRTW Interpersonal skills required for successful collaboration with colleagues and students. Preferred Qualifications Ability or interest in teaching Chaucer and a range of medieval literature. Ability to teach Shakespeare and/or other early modern literature. Ability to contribute to World Literature offerings Interest in developing interdisciplinary courses and contributing to the continued development of the Medieval Studies minor. Teaching or scholarship that expands students’ understanding of medieval and early modern literary cultures through global, comparative, interdisciplinary, or innovative approaches to texts and storytelling. Employment Conditions This is a full-time, tenure-track appointment requiring daily on-campus contact; instructional duties during the academic year (9 months, Aug. 16 – May 15) come with the option to teach during the summers for additional compensation. Application Instructions Applicants should submit 1) a letter of application stating candidate’s qualifications for the position and teaching philosophy; 2) a current CV including the names, phone numbers, and email address of three professional references from whom letters of recommendation can be requested; 3) a sample of representative writing (an article, or chapter-length contribution); and 4) all graduate transcripts (unofficial are acceptable for initial review). All materials should be submitted by October 19, 2026. Please contact Dr. Amanda Hiner at hinera@winthrop.edu with questions. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Open Date 09/15/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0016P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty-Letter of application and statement of teaching philosophy Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22872": {
      "title": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Budget Manager Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Budget Manager Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Budget Manager State Title Accountant/Fiscal Analyst III State Class Code AD25 State Pay Table GEN Annual Salary/Hiring Range $77,356 Job Purpose Reporting to the Associate Vice President for Finance & Business, the Budget Manager develops and maintains the university’s budgets, analyzes budget and financial data and trends, and prepares regular and ad hoc reports. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in accounting or similar with at least 15 semester hours in accounting-related courses. An equivalent combination of education and experience may be considered. At least five years of relevant experience. Proficiency with Microsoft Excel. Preferred Qualifications Relevant experience at an institution of higher education. Proficiency with Ellucian’s Banner software. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Summary Working knowledge of the principles and best practices of budgeting and financial management. Knowledge of accounting principles and best practices and state governmental processes. Ability to analyze budget data and exercise independent judgment in making related decisions. Ability to exercise discretion in dealing with confidential information. Exceptional interpersonal skills and the ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with faculty, staff, students and the general public. Effective oral and written communication skills. Sufficient technical ability to master required software. Job Summary Budget Development Leads the budget development process, including communication, training, budget committee coordination and execution, and budget compilation. Analyzes submitted budgets for reasonableness and to ensure opportunities are maximized. Reconciles loaded budget to board-approved budget. Budget Analysis Maintains a variety of reports, including scenario modeling, benchmarking, capital renewal schedules, fringe benefits, course and lab fee reasonableness, etc. Shares timely communication on trends requiring attention. Budget Reporting and Documentation Files annual state reports. Prepares cabinet and board-level reports. Prepares ad hoc budget reports. Advises Business Center staff regarding departmental budget reporting, as needed. Creates and maintains procedural documentation. Budget Transactional Coverage Serves comprehensively as a backup for all entry and roll-forward activities. Serves comprehensively as backup for epaf reviews and approvals. Serves comprehensively as backup for vacancy and position maintenance. Other Collaborates on continuous improvement projects. Travel/Overtime Requirements Telecommuting Employees are not eligible for telecommuting/remote work during their 12-month probationary period. After that time, certain positions may be eligible for telecommuting/remote work at the discretion of the supervisor and in accordance with Winthrop policies. License/Certification Type of Position Classified Position Status Full-Time FLSA Status Exempt Hours Per Week 37.5 Months per Year 12 Posting Detail Information Posting Number 2027027S Open Date 09/15/2026 Close Date 09/21/2026 Special Instructions to Applicants PLEASE NOTE * A resume will not be reviewed to determine whether an applicant meets the qualifications outlined in the job vacancy notice (posting). * Responses to supplemental questions are considered part of the application; failure to respond will result in disqualification. NOTICE Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Describe your experience preparing and presenting budget and financial reports. (Open Ended Question) * Rate your proficiency with Excel and indicate how you have used spreadsheets in your current or previous position. (Open Ended Question) Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22875": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Anthropology",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor, Anthropology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor, Anthropology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Full consideration will be given to applications received on or before November 2, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered if a suitable candidate has not been found. Applicants should submit: (1) a cover letter that describes ways the candidate can contribute to Winthrop's curriculum and a planned research trajectory. (2) A curriculum vitae that includes names, phone numbers and email addresses for three professional references. (3) A teaching statement with the option to include up to two course evaluations. (4) transcripts of coursework that includes at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment). References of semifinalists will be contacted by the committee after an initial screening. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Please address questions to Dr. Brent Woodfill, Search Committee Chair: woodfillb@winthrop.edu Dr. Brad Tripp, Department Chair, and Patricia Ossers De Vasquez, Department Administrative Assistant, are also available to assist with questions at trippb@winthrop.edu and ossersdevasquezp@winthrop.edu . Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor, Anthropology Job Description The Winthrop University Department of Sociology, Criminology, and Anthropology invites applications for a 9-month tenure-track position beginning August 16th, 2027. The successful candidate will have an active research agenda and be able to teach introductory courses in anthropology and courses in their area of specialization. The successful candidate will join a thriving multidisciplinary department that includes majors and concentrations in anthropology, sociology, anthropology, social inequalities, criminology, pre-law, user experience (UX) research, and criminal justice. The preferred candidate will demonstrate potential for teaching and research involving students from diverse ethnic, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds. Preferred candidates will also display a research agenda in urban anthropology or forensic anthropology. We welcome applicants who conduct research in any geographical area. We seek a broadly trained teacher-scholar-mentor interested in involving undergraduate students in various forms of experiential learning including research, internships, and community engagement. The university actively encourages applications from candidates in groups that have not historically been well represented in higher education. The department supports a thriving B.A. program in Sociology with concentrations in criminology, social inequalities, and anthropology as well as a new B.A. in Criminal Justice with a track in cyber and corporate crime investigations. The successful applicant will have the opportunity to build connections with other centers and units on campus, including the newly created Center for the Environment and the Health Science program. Position Responsibilities he preferred candidate will demonstrate the potential to teach and conduct research involving students from an array of cultural and socioeconomic backgrounds. We prefer candidates who have expertise in urban anthropology or foresncie anthropology. Major responsibilities include: Teaching the equivalent of twelve contact hours each semester that includes both introductory and advanced courses within their areas of expertise. Contribute to and deliver methods and career preparation courses in the Sociology, Criminology, and Anthropology department. Pursue a productive research agenda that promotes the engagement of undergraduates All faculty are expected to contribute to student advising, efforts towards recruitment and retention, as well as college and university committee service. Participating in professional service commensurate with rank. Overall expectation for teaching, scholarship and service for an Assistant Professor can be found in the College of Arts and Sciences Faculty Handbook: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/faculty.aspx About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Anthropology is required for appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor ( ABD will be considered with the expectation of having Ph.D. in hand by time of hiring). Potential for teaching excellence and commitment to student learning. Evidence of ongoing program of research and the potential for publication, ideally in urban or forensic anthropology. Interpersonal skills required for successful collaboration with colleagues and students. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D in Anthropology with a specialization in urban or forensic anthropology. Ability to teach topics related to social inequalities (Such as Race, Class, Gender, Sexuality) is a plus, as is a research agenda focused on the broader social and cultural dimensions of crime, justice, and victimhood. Experience mentoring undergraduate research including, but not limited to, research lab supervision, coauthoring with students, and bringing students to conferences. Experience mentoring undergraduates in internships. Employment Conditions This is a full-time, tenure-track appointment requiring daily on-campus contact; instructional duties during the academic year (9 months, Aug. 16 – May 15) come with the option to teach during the summers for additional compensation. Application Instructions Full consideration will be given to applications received on or before November 2, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered if a suitable candidate has not been found. Applicants should submit: (1) a cover letter that describes ways the candidate can contribute to Winthrop’s curriculum and a planned research trajectory. (2) A curriculum vitae that includes names, phone numbers and email addresses for three professional references. (3) A teaching statement with the option to include up to two course evaluations. (4) transcripts of coursework that includes at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment). References of semifinalists will be contacted by the committee after an initial screening. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Please address questions to Dr. Brent Woodfill, Search Committee Chair: woodfillb@winthrop.edu Dr. Brad Tripp, Department Chair, and Patricia Ossers De Vasquez, Department Administrative Assistant, are also available to assist with questions at trippb@winthrop.edu and ossersdevasquezp@winthrop.edu . Open Date 09/15/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0018P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22871": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Psychology",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor, Psychology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor, Psychology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested candidates should submit the following materials for consideration before midnight on November 9, 2026: Letter of interest, with evidence of major responsibilities and required qualifications A statement of teaching philosophy Evidence of excellence in teaching and commitment to student learning Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested. All graduate transcripts (demonstrating at least 18hrs in the discipline) and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment) Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of all references.) For more information, please contact Donna Nelson at nelsond@winthrop.edu. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor, Psychology Job Description The Department of Psychology at Winthrop University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position specializing in developmental psychology or a closely related area. The successful candidate will teach, supervise, and advise psychology students while maintaining a student-centered research program. Specializations may include aging, adulthood, adolescence, childhood or development across the lifespan. We’re particularly interested in candidates whose teaching and scholarship will expand the department’s developmental psychology offerings and support students pursuing careers or graduate study in fields such as gerontology, healthcare, behavioral health, counseling, education, child life, and occupational therapy. Position Responsibilities Teach undergraduate courses across the discipline that include introductory psychology, research methods, and statistics for psychology majors. Teach additional undergraduate psychology courses including developmental psychology and other courses within the candidate’s area of expertise Establish a vibrant research program that involves undergraduates Support the program by engaging in curriculum design and student advising Engage in professional stewardship across the campus to improve the overall Winthrop community About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Doctorate in psychology. ABD candidates will be considered and appointed at the rank of instructor until the doctorate is completed Evidence of teaching effectiveness within higher education Evidence of scholarly activity and potential for research Ability to engage and support students from a range of backgrounds and experiences Strong skills in innovative teaching technologies. Familiarity with such programs as Excel, Qualtrics, and online teaching platforms is advantageous Evidence of potential for successful collaboration with colleagues and students Preferred Qualifications Area of specialization in aging or gerontology Employment Conditions This is full-time, tenure-track appointment requiring daily on-campus contact; instructional duties during the academic year (9 months, Aug. 16 – May 15) come with the option to teach during the summers for additional compensation. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit the following materials for consideration before midnight on November 9, 2026: Letter of interest, with evidence of major responsibilities and required qualifications A statement of teaching philosophy Evidence of excellence in teaching and commitment to student learning Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested. All graduate transcripts (demonstrating at least 18hrs in the discipline) and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment) Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of all references.) For more information, please contact Donna Nelson at nelsond@winthrop.edu. Open Date 09/15/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0017P PositionType: Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty - Letter of Interest Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Faculty-Evidence of teaching excellence Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
